## Why This Is Asked

This question tests practical replay-protection patterns in multi-tenant SSO architectures, along with real-world token normalization from OIDC and SAML protocols, JWKS key rotation, and designing scalable validation endpoints.

## Key Concepts

- Token validation across OIDC and SAML standards
- Per-tenant JWKS rotation and SAML metadata caching
- Nonce-based replay protection with time-to-live (TTL) enforcement
- Internal JWT issuance including tenant_id, user_id, scopes, and nonce

## Code Example

```javascript
// Issue internal token with nonce (Node.js)
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');

async function issueInternalToken({ tenant_id, user_id, scopes, nonce }) {
  const payload = {
    tenant_id,
    sub: user_id,
    scopes,
    nonce,
    iat: Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000),
    exp: Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000) + 300 // 5-minute expiry
  };
  return jwt.sign(payload, getInternalSigningKey(tenant_id), { algorithm: 'RS256' });
}

// Validate nonce during token validation
async function validateAndConsumeNonce(tenant_id, nonce) {
  const key = `nonce:${tenant_id}:${nonce}`;
  const exists = await redis.setnx(key, '1');
  if (!exists) {
    throw new Error('Replay detected: nonce already used');
  }
  await redis.expire(key, 300); // Match token TTL
  return true;
}

// /validate endpoint - lightweight claim extraction
app.post('/validate', async (req, res) => {
  const { token } = req.body;
  const [header] = token.split('.');
  const decodedHeader = JSON.parse(Buffer.from(header, 'base64'));
  const tenant_id = decodedHeader.tenant_id;

  const claims = await extractClaimsWithoutFullDecode(token, tenant_id);
  return res.json({ valid: true, claims });
});
```

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to design cross-protocol schema alignment and implement practical versioning strategies.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-protocol schema mapping from canonical model
- Unified internal service layer with protocol adapters
- Consistent versioning across all protocols
- Contract validation and routing patterns

## Code Example

```javascript
// Canonical model adapter
function toInternalBook(restInput) {
  return {
    id: restInput.id || generateId(),
    title: restInput.title,
    author: restInput.author,
    stock: restInput.stock || 0
  };
}

// Protocol-agnostic service layer
const bookService = {
  async getBook(id) {
    return await database.books.findById(id);
  },
  async createBook(bookData) {
    const internalBook = toInternalBook(bookData);
    return await database.books.create(internalBook);
  }
};

// Protocol adapters
const restAdapter = {
  getBook: async (req, res) => {
    const book = await bookService.getBook(req.params.id);
    res.json(book);
  },
  createBook: async (req, res) => {
    const book = await bookService.createBook(req.body);
    res.status(201).json(book);
  }
};

// GraphQL schema
type Book {
  id: ID!
  title: String!
  author: String!
  stock: Int!
}

type Query {
  getBook(id: ID!): Book
}

type Mutation {
  addBook(title: String!, author: String!, stock: Int): Book
}

// gRPC service definition
service BookService {
  rpc GetBook(GetBookRequest) returns (Book);
  rpc CreateBook(CreateBookRequest) returns (Book);
}

// Versioning patterns
// REST: /api/v1/books
// GraphQL: Use @deprecated directive for v2 changes
// gRPC: package v1.bookservice;
```

## Implementation Strategy
1. Define canonical domain model first
2. Create protocol-specific schemas that map to the canonical model
3. Implement adapter layer for request/response transformation
4. Build unified service layer with business logic
5. Apply consistent versioning strategy across all protocols

